vtpm-mds-reconcile for the SPIRE server host
--------------------------------------------

This package installs the reconciler (/usr/sbin/vtpm-mds-reconcile), its
systemd unit, the system user vtpm-mds-reconcile and an example
configuration under /usr/share/doc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/examples/. Install it
once, on the host of the SPIRE server, not on Proxmox nodes and not in
guests. What it does, and why a tag grants only inside a pool, is in
vtpm-mds-reconcile(8).

It ships no configuration in /etc. The unit is enabled on install but starts
only once /etc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/config.yaml exists; before that a start is
skipped, not failed. An upgrade restarts a running reconciler and leaves a
stopped one stopped.

1. Configuration and files
   Start from the example:

     install -d -m 0750 -o root -g vtpm-mds-reconcile /etc/vtpm-mds-reconcile
     install -m 0640 -o root -g vtpm-mds-reconcile \
       /usr/share/doc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/examples/config.yaml \
       /etc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/config.yaml

   Put each cluster's ca_file and intermediates beside it, 0640
   root:vtpm-mds-reconcile, so the service user can read them. The
   intermediates are certificates only, never keys.

2. Tokens, as systemd credentials
   Each cluster needs a Proxmox API token with PVEAuditor on / for the user
   and the token. Keep each token file root:root 0600 and hand it over with
   one LoadCredential= line per cluster in a drop-in; the configuration's
   token_file then points at /run/credentials/vtpm-mds-reconcile.service/:

     # systemctl edit vtpm-mds-reconcile
     [Service]
     LoadCredential=cluster-1.token:/etc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/cluster-1.token

   The token is read once at start; a rotated token needs a restart.

3. The SPIRE admin socket
   SPIRE 1.14 creates its admin socket 0770 and the two directories above it
   0750, owned by the server's user and primary group. Give the reconciler
   that group in the same drop-in:

     SupplementaryGroups=spire

   (use the group the SPIRE server actually runs as). Whoever opens the
   socket is a SPIRE admin, so this group is the reconciler's whole SPIRE
   authority.

   The unit runs with PrivateTmp=true, which hides SPIRE's default socket
   /tmp/spire-server/private/api.sock. Either set the SPIRE server's
   socket_path under /run (recommended, as in the example), or, for a server
   that stays on the default path, add to the drop-in:

     PrivateTmp=false

   A socket path holds at most 107 bytes.

4. First start

     systemctl start vtpm-mds-reconcile
     journalctl -u vtpm-mds-reconcile

   The log names the version and the number of clusters, then each pass's
   changes. A token without VM.Audit on /vms or Pool.Audit on /pool, or a
   socket it cannot open, stops the start with a line naming the cluster or
   the socket; systemd retries every 10 s and gives up after five starts in
   five minutes. The unit is then failed and refuses a plain start until
   those five minutes are over; after fixing the cause:

     systemctl reset-failed vtpm-mds-reconcile
     systemctl start vtpm-mds-reconcile

   The unit is not ordered after the SPIRE server, whose unit name differs
   per installation. If SPIRE runs on the same host and may take longer than
   a minute to open its socket at boot, add to the drop-in:

     [Unit]
     After=spire-server.service

   (with the SPIRE server's actual unit name).

   To try a configuration without the service: run
   vtpm-mds-reconcile -once -config <file> as a user who can read the token
   files and open the socket. -once never bans.

Removing the package stops the service and leaves the aliases it wrote in
SPIRE; delete them by hand if the reconciler is not coming back. Purging it
leaves /etc/vtpm-mds-reconcile/ and the token files, which the operator
created and the package does not own.
